---
title: Package Cache
order: 6
description: -Sc, -Scc, paccache, cache directory
tags: [pacman, cache, clean, paccache, disk]
---

Managing the local package cache to free disk space while retaining
rollback capability.

@code bash
# ---- cache location ----

ls /var/cache/pacman/pkg/                 # default cache directory
du -sh /var/cache/pacman/pkg/             # cache size

@code bash
# ---- pacman built-in cleanup ----

pacman -Sc                                # remove uninstalled package caches
pacman -Scc                               # remove ALL cached packages (nuclear)

@text
`pacman -Sc` removes cached packages that are no longer installed. This
is safe but prevents downgrading to previous versions of removed packages.

`pacman -Scc` removes everything from the cache, including packages you
currently have installed. This means you cannot downgrade without
re-downloading. Use sparingly.

@code bash
# ---- paccache: fine-grained cache control (recommended) ----

# install
pacman -S pacman-contrib

# remove all but last 3 versions of each package (default)
paccache -r

# keep only last 1 version
paccache -rk 1

# remove all versions of uninstalled packages
paccache -ruk0

# dry run: show what would be removed
paccache -d
paccache -dk 1

# show cache statistics
paccache -v

# remove packages older than 30 days (custom)
find /var/cache/pacman/pkg/ -mtime +30 -name "*.pkg.tar.*" -delete

@code bash
# ---- automate cache cleanup ----

# create a pacman hook to run paccache after every upgrade
# /etc/pacman.d/hooks/clean_cache.hook

[Trigger]
Operation = Upgrade
Operation = Install
Operation = Remove
Type = Package
Target = *

[Action]
Description = Cleaning pacman cache (keeping last 3)...
When = PostTransaction
Exec = /usr/bin/paccache -rk 3

@code bash
# ---- download packages without installing ----

pacman -Sw package                        # download to cache only
pacman -Sw --cachedir /tmp/pkgs package   # download to custom location

# download an entire repo for offline use
pacman -Syw $(pacman -Ssq) --cachedir /mnt/usb/pkgs/

@text
Recommendation: Use `paccache -rk 2` periodically to keep the last 2
versions of every package. This balances disk usage against the ability
to roll back one version if an upgrade causes problems. The pacman hook
above automates this.
